This repository is published so that the results in README.md can be checked and
the engineering behind them can be read. That is its whole purpose, and reading
it is explicitly welcome.

It is not open source. No licence is granted to use, copy, modify, merge,
publish, distribute, sublicense or sell this software, or works derived from it,
in whole or in part. The absence of a permissive licence here is deliberate
rather than an oversight.

Two things are permitted beyond reading. Reproducing the measurements, which is
the point of publishing them: every raw response is committed, so `drift replay`
and `drift report` regenerate every published figure from disk without calling
any vendor and without spending anything. And the forking that GitHub's terms of
service already extend to anyone who can see a public repository.

If you want to use any of this for anything else, please ask first by opening an
issue on this repository. The answer is likely to be yes.

Two things in here are not mine to license at all.

The suite draws on public evaluation sets. Every item records its origin and that
origin's own licence in its `source` and `licence` fields, item by item, so the
terms attached to any given question can be read off the question. Across the 400
public items those are MIT (220), CC0 (70), CC-BY-SA-4.0 (40), Apache-2.0 (30),
CC-BY-4.0 (20) and public domain (20). Those terms govern those items, including
where they ask for attribution or share-alike, and this notice does not override
them. See docs/sampling.md for which set each block was drawn from.

The public-domain 20 are the long-context passages, taken from Project Gutenberg
with its boilerplate removed. See docs/long-context.md.

Third-party dependencies keep their own licences, which are recorded in
pyproject.toml and resolved in uv.lock. Nothing above applies to them.
